From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: wize-logic <bcolb7@protonmail.com>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 10:22:24 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] recomp: --stack-locals keeps a function's own stack slots in
 C locals

Compiled x86 code keeps its locals and spills in its stack frame, and the
lifter writes each access there as a guest memory access: a byte-reversed
load or store on a big-endian host. The compiler forwards a stored value to
the next load of the same slot only where nothing between may alias it, and
never drops a store; any store through a pointer or any call ends it.

stack_locals.py, run on each body before x87_locals, follows esp (pushes,
pops, esp arithmetic, each callee's effect from preserve.py) and the values
that are addresses in the function's own frame (esp + K, ebp after
`mov ebp, esp`, a register loaded from a slot that holds one), so that every
access to the frame lands on a known slot. A slot that only same-width
integer or float accesses reach (no push or pop, no SSE or 80-bit access, at
least two accesses) becomes a C local: loaded at the entry and after each
call (plain loads the compiler drops where the local is written first),
written back before each call when it may have changed, and before a return
when it belongs to the caller (the arguments). The slots below the entry esp
are not written back at a return.

A function keeps the memory model when an access lands at an offset the pass
cannot know (a local array indexed by a register, esp after an indirect call
that cleans the stack itself), when a frame address is stored outside the
frame or passed to anything but an access or a guest call, when a callee
given a frame address returns a pointer that is then used, or at setjmp, a
switch or a form it does not follow. It assumes, beyond preserve.py, that a
pointer the function did not make from esp or ebp does not point into its
frame, and that a callee reads its caller's frame only through its arguments
and the pointers it was given.

test_stack_locals.py lifts small functions: slots kept, an indexed access and
a frame address stored outside the frame that must not be converted, and
four programs compiled and run both ways with the same results.
